                <pb facs="0104" n="16" sameAs="frus:frus1904/pg16"/>

                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Inclosure.—Translation.]</seg></opener>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">From the Moniteur Beige, February 12, 1904</hi></head>
                <p rendition="#center"><hi rendition="#small-caps">official notices.</hi></p>
                <p>Russia and Japan being in a state of war, the Government reminds the people that
                    Belgium is perpetually neutral, and that any act contrary to the duties of
                    neutrality must be carefully avoided.</p>
                <p>The penal code contains the following provision, to which it may be useful to
                    call public attention:</p>
                <p><hi rendition="#small-caps">Art</hi>. 123. Whosoever, by hostile actions not
                    approved of by the Government, shall have exposed the state to hostilities on
                    the part of a foreign power, shall be punished with imprisonment from five to
                    ten years, and if hostilities should result therefrom, with ten to fifteen
                    years’ imprisonment.</p>
            </div>
